    fleet/pick <N> [--dry-run] [--json]    pick (and claim) one ticket for Runner N
    fleet/pick --check-access              does every Runner N login sit on boards 15, 2060, 11514?
    fleet/pick --wip                       read only: wip: N of M, the units counted and the ones left out
    fleet/pick --replay-waits [DAYS]       offline: runs the wait gate would have held, from the run ledger

Valentin 2026-11-07: one pool, Runner 1..N, each runner on the engine the pacer gives it (engine-N, else the engine file,
else cursor): cursor (Cursor plan), codex (hax on Codex), opencode (hax on OpenCode Go) or claude (Claude Code on the Claude plan). The model per brain tag (and for
park and incident runs) comes from the pacer's ~/.local/state/pacing/allocation.json when it is under an hour old; else the
built-in ENGINES map, which is the house-rules brain map. A brain the runner's engine skips is never handed to it, and a
ticket keeps its first runner, engine and model in STATE/ticket-owners until Done. Temporary stops, engine changes,
expired claims and parks never make it an orphan. Only a permanently removed roster slot permits recovery. This replaces ht-or-runner-pick,
ci-pick, infra-pick, mp-pick and mb-pick (verbatim copies live in fleet/legacy/). Order:
  0. a live incident (YPER4-1375): an open board 15 ticket labelled incident that no live runner works. Any runner
     takes it first, whatever it holds or its engine: the run is Cursor grok-4.7 high (house rule), even on a Codex slot. Lane cap, app freeze, rests, queue brake and holds do not apply (ship-claim runs with
     SHIP_CLAIM_INCIDENT=1). Model grok-4.7-high, brief briefs/incident.txt in front of the app brief. A runner assigned to
     an incident it is not running right now (log) is handed it again; an incident another runner is running is left alone.
  a. my own ticket in a building column on any board, QA included (the merging runner does the live check; a design-pass
     QA ticket waits for another runner's cross-qa)
  b. an orphan in a building column, QA included (no durable owner and nobody holds it, or its roster slot was removed)
  WIP cap: the pool's pipeline (building columns on every fleet board plus new-work tickets with an open PR, one unit
     per ticket, minus incidents, open BlockedBy, waits on a person, design-pass and work outside the pool) at or over
     the live runners closes c0, d, e and f. A ticket nobody holds counts only when a free runner can take it: not in a
     run, not already holding a unit, able to do its brain, and a checks slot free when the ticket is checks work.
     A full checks lane, or a brain only a busy runner can do, does not fill the cap (HYCI-232). Then only the PR queue
     and new-work tickets with an open PR remain.
      Logged as "wip: N of M, new work closed". The cap never holds back the release pipeline: at the cap, Fleet CI & Infra (11514)
     tickets that are Urgent or that an open ticket is BlockedBy still go out, to runners that can join 11514 (LANE_CAP applies).
  Kept slot (HYCI-186): while no other runner is on a Fleet CI & Infra (11514) ticket, this runner takes
     one before its own app ticket, before orphans and before the PR queue. The WIP cap and LANE_CAP do not hold that one
     ticket. The stderr line starts with "kept slot:". App work only when no such ticket is open.
  c. Urgent new work on any board, then the PR queue (pr-drain-pick rules)
  d. CI or infra work that is Urgent or blocks an app ticket, while fewer than LANE_CAP runners hold CI or infra work
  e. new work by priority (Urgent, High, Medium, Low) across boards; same priority: board 15 (ai-feedback included), then 2060, then 11514
  f. the rest of the CI or infra lane, same cap
One runner holds one ticket until it is live. brain-extra-hard runs on the codex engine (Sol high) or the claude engine (Opus high); every other engine skips it.
A ticket any runner ended IDLE on rests for every runner (runner-N.idle files and finished runs) until the ticket changes
(updated after the IDLE end) or for SHORT_REST (2 hours) when it waits on nobody (no Valentin wait), else 3 days at most;
a ticket with an open BlockedBy link is held by that link; a closed link does not lift a rest (the blocker's fix changes the ticket).
A ticket with an open BlockedBy link, in Valentin Review or waiting on someone is never picked (fleet/park puts them there).
That someone may be Valentin or the board lead: fleet/park --lead keeps the starting runner assigned while the lead resolves the wait. A ticket a lead or another agent holds is not new work on Fleet CI & Infra either, nor is one
waiting on an agent (HYCI-276: a lead-parked board alarm came back to four runners).
Wait gate (13 Nov): a ticket whose last run ended WAIT_CHECK, or FAULT naming CI, a PR or a blocker, is also held from every lane
that reads resting() (own, orphan, kept slot, board, PR queue, incident, the pipeline count) until what it waits on changes: the PR
head commit, review decision, mergeable state, or its checks (read as "running" until the last one finishes or one fails, so a
check finishing under seven still running is not news), a BlockedBy ticket or its column, or the ticket itself (edited more than a
minute after the end). The fingerprint is kept at the first pick after the end in STATE/wait-gate.json and read from data the
pick already holds, so it costs no GitHub or board call. Cap 6 hours (Urgent 1 hour, incident 20 minutes); a wait with nothing to
observe is held until its stated recheck time, else 30 minutes. A run that built and left the ticket in AI Review or QA is held
too while its PR's checks run at the first pick (canary HYCI-358: the next run only found them running). Unreadable or late data, and any error, hold nothing.
fleet/pick --replay-waits [DAYS] counts what it would have held in the run ledger. The park lane is not gated.
One ticket, one run, one PR (13 Nov): a ticket whose open PR has pending or green checks, no conflict and no change request
is held from every lane (through resting()) and logged as "one-run: <KEY> PR open, checks pending|green"; a script owns the check
watch, the merge and the live check (HYCI-352), and a model wakes on the ticket only when the PR is red, conflicting or has a
change request. Read from the shared open-PR list, so no extra GitHub call. Live only when ~/.local/state/fleet/one-run.on exists:
no script merges green hypertask-ai/hypertask PRs before HYCI-352 ships, so until then the line is a shadow log and nothing is
held. A PR untouched for 6 hours (Urgent 1) is released. An incident is never held. Flag or not, the orphan lane skips a ticket whose open PR checks are still running (HYCI-358: two orphan parks in two minutes). fleet/runner refuses a launch with no ticket key.
Cannot be built yet (HYCI-355): a new-work ticket that says it needs Valentin, a key only he or an admin can place, access,
a design sign-off or a person at a PC is refused before any model starts ("cannot be built yet: <what>"). Text only, no
board read. An incident and a ticket with an open PR are never held. bin/replay-built [DAYS] counts the first runs since
and how many of them the hold refuses.
A durable owner's waiting ticket stays held without starting a park-only model run. When the wait clears, that same runner
resumes it from its current column before taking any new ticket. Legacy tickets without durable ownership retain the park-job path.
ship-claim runs with SHIP_CLAIM_PICKED=1: this picker already ordered infra against app work, so its infra-first hold is skipped.
--dry-run never claims and never writes under ~/.local/state; it asks `ship-claim hold` (read only) so it sees the same holds. FLEET_PICK_VIRTUAL=<file> makes dry runs
share virtual claims (so 20 separate dry runs give 20 different tickets); FLEET_PICK_CACHE=<file> reuses one
board snapshot. Exit 0 with a pick, exit 1 with the reason on stderr.
